    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8ab2ae19-9d24-50a1-86ea-63d0a9a1a2e6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "79e260b2-55c0-5429-8a40-8e49a11e9d05",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
