      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "A hair straightener reaches a temperature of ${450}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning it off, the temperature $T(t)$ of the straightener decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the straightener temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$",
